  "question": {
    "text": "Suppose the gravitational force varies inversely as the nth power of distance. Then the time period of a planet in circular orbit of radius ' $\\mathrm{R}$ ' around the sun will be proportional to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "JKmBY",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{n}}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Qg85O",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{R}^{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{n}-1}{2}\\right)}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s9QxV",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{R}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{n}+1}{2}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "bs6pV",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{R}^{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{n}-2}{2}\\right)}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$$\n\\mathrm{F}=\\mathrm{KR}^{-\\mathrm{n}}=\\mathrm{MR} \\omega^{2} \\Rightarrow \\omega^{2}=\\mathrm{KR}^{-(\\mathrm{n}+1)}\n$$\nor $\\omega=\\mathrm{K}^{\\prime} \\mathrm{R}^{\\frac{-(\\mathrm{n}+1)}{2}}$\n[where $\\mathrm{K}^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{K}^{1 / 2}$, a constant $]$\n$\\frac{2 \\pi}{T} \\alpha R^{\\frac{-(n+1)}{2}} \\quad \\therefore T \\alpha R^{\\frac{(n+1)}{2}}$",
    "image": null
  },
